Falsifies `machine_discovery::framebuffer::verification::every_decimal_field_the_loader_writes_is_one_the_kernel_reads_back`.

The defect: `parse_decimal` accumulates with a plain multiply and add instead of
the checked pair.

`u32::MAX` is ten digits and `write_decimal`'s buffer is exactly ten bytes, so the
encoder has no slack and the decoder's overflow boundary has to sit exactly at
`4294967295` accepted and `4294967296` refused. With the checks gone the decoder
does not refuse the second one, it wraps it, and a stride or a height read off a
boot command line becomes a different number than the one written, with nothing
saying so.

Under Kani the wrap is a caught arithmetic overflow rather than a silent one, which
is the point: the same expression in a release kernel wraps quietly.

Expected to fail: the arithmetic-overflow checks on `value * 10 + digit`, and
behind them `assert_eq!(parse_decimal(text), Some(value))`.

diff --git a/crates/machine_discovery/src/framebuffer.rs b/crates/machine_discovery/src/framebuffer.rs
index 50e1dcfb..9bf50744 100644
--- a/crates/machine_discovery/src/framebuffer.rs
+++ b/crates/machine_discovery/src/framebuffer.rs
@@ -307,7 +307,7 @@ fn parse_decimal(text: &str) -> Option<u32> {
     let mut value = 0u32;
     for byte in text.bytes() {
         let digit = byte.checked_sub(b'0').filter(|d| *d < 10)?;
-        value = value.checked_mul(10)?.checked_add(u32::from(digit))?;
+        value = value * 10 + u32::from(digit);
     }
     Some(value)
 }
